cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
type: article
title: "When Is Model Simplification Safe? Consequence and Detectability for Common-Shape Weibull Series Systems under Masked Data"
authors:
  - family-names: Towell
    given-names: Alexander
    email: lex@metafunctor.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6443-9897"
    affiliation: "Southern Illinois University Edwardsville"
abstract: >-
  A reliability engineer modeling a Weibull series system must choose between
  a full model with a separate shape parameter for each component and a
  parsimonious reduced model that assumes a common shape. We show that the two
  questions a practitioner cares about, how much accuracy the simplification
  costs and whether the data can detect that it is wrong, are structurally
  decoupled. The reduced model's prediction bias is a sample-size-independent
  population quantity that grows as the square of the shape coefficient of
  variation, while the likelihood ratio test's detection power is governed by
  a noncentrality parameter that scales with the sample size times the same
  quantity, so the detectable threshold falls as one over the square root of
  the sample size. We prove a conjugacy identity: at any common-shape point
  the common-shape constraint yields exactly zero first-order efficiency gain
  for any functional of the system lifetime distribution. The identity forces
  the misspecification bias to be second-order in heterogeneity and explains
  why, at moderate sample sizes, the full model can have lower mean
  time-to-failure mean squared error than the reduced model even when the
  common shape is exactly correct. The practical result is a two-regime safety
  rule, derived under right-censored and masked failure data.
keywords:
  - reliability
  - series systems
  - masked data
  - Weibull distribution
  - model selection
  - likelihood ratio test
  - model misspecification
  - focused information criterion
  - pretest estimation
  - constrained maximum likelihood
